14	/// Checks for mutable objects passed as a value argument to `dict.fromkeys`.
15	///
16	/// ## Why is this bad?
17	/// All values in the dictionary created by the `dict.fromkeys` method
18	/// refer to the same instance of the provided object. If that object is
19	/// modified, all values are modified, which can lead to unexpected behavior.
20	/// For example, if the empty list (`[]`) is provided as the default value,
21	/// all values in the dictionary will use the same list; as such, appending to
22	/// any one entry will append to all entries.
23	///
24	/// Instead, use a comprehension to generate a dictionary with distinct
25	/// instances of the default value.
26	///
27	/// ## Example
28	/// ```python
29	/// cities = dict.fromkeys(["UK", "Poland"], [])
30	/// cities["UK"].append("London")
31	/// cities["Poland"].append("Poznan")
32	/// print(cities)  # {'UK': ['London', 'Poznan'], 'Poland': ['London', 'Poznan']}
33	/// ```
34	///
35	/// Use instead:
36	/// ```python
37	/// cities = {country: [] for country in ["UK", "Poland"]}
38	/// cities["UK"].append("London")
39	/// cities["Poland"].append("Poznan")
40	/// print(cities)  # {'UK': ['London'], 'Poland': ['Poznan']}
